```c
// Thread 1                      // Thread 2
lock(&mutexA);                   lock(&mutexB);
// ... does some work ...        // ... does some work ...
lock(&mutexB);                   lock(&mutexA);
// ... critical section ...      // ... critical section ...
unlock(&mutexB);                 unlock(&mutexA);
unlock(&mutexA);                 unlock(&mutexB);
```

If Thread 1 acquires `mutexA` and Thread 2 acquires `mutexB` at roughly the same time, Thread 1 then blocks trying to get `mutexB` (held by Thread 2), and Thread 2 blocks trying to get `mutexA` (held by Thread 1). Neither will ever release what it holds, because releasing happens only after the critical section, which neither can reach. This tiny, realistic-looking snippet has all four Coffman conditions present at once — it's worth being able to recognize this exact shape instantly.
